      "beaver_desc": "The defining engineer of New England wetlands. Their dams raise water tables, slow flooding, create wet meadows, and create firebreaks. Recovering from near-extinction in the 1800s fur trade.",
      "boomObserver_desc": "See prey population exceed 150",
      "brookTrout_desc": "Maine state fish. Native to cold, clean streams. The Northeast holds the largest remaining wild brook trout populations in the continental United States. Climate warming and habitat fragmentation are the central threats.",
      "carryCapExplorer_desc": "Change carrying capacity 3 times",
      "cascadeMaster_desc": "Trigger a full trophic cascade (wolves up, deer down, habitat recovers) in the Conservation Manager",
      "coexist_desc": "Both species above 5 for 100 samples",
      "deer_desc": "Hyperabundant in much of southern New England since predators were removed. Heavy browse pressure prevents oak and pine regeneration. In Maine the picture is mixed: dense in the south and along the coast, sparse in the deep north woods.",
      "directorRank_desc": "Complete a 10-year Conservation Manager campaign on Director difficulty",
      "director_desc": "18 hours / year, harsher events. Tight constraint.",
      "eventSurvivor_desc": "Trigger 3 different events",
      "explanation_input": "Ecosystem trade-off explanation",
      "extinctionWitness_desc": "Witness a population crash to below 5",
      "firstSim_desc": "Run your first graph simulation",
      "graphToggler_desc": "Switch between Population and Environment graph views",
      "grayWolf_desc": "Locally extirpated in Maine for over a century. Reintroduction is the most dramatic conservation move possible: documented in Yellowstone to trigger a trophic cascade by reducing elk browse, letting streamside willows and aspen recover. (Whether wolves also reshaped stream channels themselves is still debated among scientists.)",
      "manager_desc": "24 hours / year, standard events. Default.",
      "moose_desc": "Maine's iconic megaherbivore and a key prey species for any returning wolf population. Numbers have declined sharply since 2014 due to winter ticks, an outbreak driven by milder winters under climate change.",
      "nightOwl_desc": "Observe the simulation during nighttime",
      "phasePortrait_desc": "View the phase portrait",
      "pred_death": "Pred Death",
      "presetExplorer_desc": "Try all 4 presets",
      "prey_birth": "Prey Birth",
      "quizMaster_desc": "Answer 5 quiz questions correctly",
      "quiz_ace_desc": "Correctly answer 3 questions in the quiz",
      "salmonChampion_desc": "Bring Atlantic salmon population above 50 in the Conservation Manager",
      "salmon_desc": "Critically endangered in the Gulf of Maine. The Penobscot River is the only Maine river with consistent returning runs. Dam removal has been the key conservation lever; in 2012 and 2013 the Veazie and Great Works dams came down through the Penobscot River Restoration Project.",
      "sandboxBuilder_desc": "Place 10 entities in sandbox mode",
      "speedDemon_desc": "Run simulation at 3x speed for 30 seconds",
      "survive_crash_desc": "Prey drops below 10, then recovers above 25",
      "troutDefender_desc": "Bring brook trout population above 70 in the Conservation Manager",
      "vocab_study_desc": "Study 3 key terminology definitions",
      "wolfReintroducer_desc": "Successfully reintroduce wolves to Maine in the Conservation Manager"
